I have identified the root cause of the `action is not a function` error. The issue was that my previous attempt to suppress Genkit's noisy registration logs during hot-reloads was returning a non-callable object from the registry instead of the actual function reference.

I will now implement a robust **Global Callable Cache** pattern. This ensures that:
1.  **No more crashes**: It returns the exact same function reference from the first registration, which is guaranteed to be a function.
2.  **No more noisy logs**: It avoids calling the duplicate registration entirely when the function is already cached.
3.  **HMR Stability**: It works seamlessly with Next.js Turbopack by preserving the "first-registered" function across process lifetimes.

```typescript
// src/modules/ai/infrastructure/services/Genkit.ts

// I will replace the wrapRegistration logic with this:
const wrapRegistration = (originalFn: any, type: string) => {
  return (...args: any[]) => {
    const config = args[0];
    const name = config.name;
    const actionId = `/${type}/${name}`;

    // Return cached callable if it exists to avoid re-registration and logs
    if (callables.has(actionId)) {
      return callables.get(actionId);
    }

    // First time registration
    const result = originalFn.apply(aiInstance, args);
    callables.set(actionId, result);
    return result;
  };
};
```

